WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:06.089
There are about 150
urban mega-developments

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:08.133
branded as new cities underway,

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:11.177
and they're located in over
40 countries around the world.

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:12.846
And that's not including China,

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:15.932
where there are at least 500 new cities

00:00:15.932 --> 00:00:17.934
being built from scratch.

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:20.437
We're in the age of new cities right now.

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:22.814
This is one of them,

00:00:22.814 --> 00:00:24.482
Songdo in South Korea.

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:27.652
Looking at the city today
you could almost forget

00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:30.238
that two decades ago it was just an idea.

00:00:35.493 --> 00:00:38.413
We used to just joke around calling Songdo "Songberia"

00:00:38.413 --> 00:00:42.000
that's a combination of the word "Songdo" and "Siberia".

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.546
The master plan starts with a blank page.

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:49.758
There's not a line for a road,

00:00:49.758 --> 00:00:52.677
there's no dot for a subway stop,

00:00:52.677 --> 00:00:54.429
in this particular case

00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:56.222
after all, it was landfilled.

00:00:56.222 --> 00:01:00.060
So you're literally starting tabula rasa

00:01:01.102 --> 00:01:04.105
before there is even any terra firma.

00:01:05.815 --> 00:01:07.692
As the urban population grows

00:01:07.692 --> 00:01:09.360
so does our need for new cities.

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:12.155
And there are hopes that
Songdo could be a blueprint

00:01:12.155 --> 00:01:14.699
for other developments around the world.

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.132
But the design and construction

00:01:31.132 --> 00:01:33.718
of this $40 billion smart city

00:01:33.718 --> 00:01:35.678
hasn't been universally well-received.

00:01:37.806 --> 00:01:40.809
It's not the nicest place on earth

00:01:40.809 --> 00:01:43.103
but it's definitely one
of the best examples

00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:45.772
of new cities developed
in the last 20 years

00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:48.233
that actually have people living in here

00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:51.861
that haven't been pushed
or forced to live here.

00:01:51.861 --> 00:01:54.072
We call it Tomorrowland.

00:01:54.072 --> 00:01:55.698
Both to kind of reference this idea

00:01:55.698 --> 00:01:58.993
that it feels a little
Disney-ish and very manicured,

00:01:58.993 --> 00:02:01.746
but also to joke that like -
it'll be better tomorrow.

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:10.046
New cities have generally

00:02:10.046 --> 00:02:13.007
always been built by
governments or kingdoms.

00:02:13.007 --> 00:02:15.009
They have been built since antiquity,

00:02:15.009 --> 00:02:17.303
but there are moments of intensification.

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:20.557
So during the colonial era

00:02:20.557 --> 00:02:23.226
we were seeing dozens
and dozens of new cities

00:02:23.226 --> 00:02:24.602
being built in the colonies.

00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:27.814
And then it's cities of the 1950s, 1960s,

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:29.607
like Chandigarh and Brasilia

00:02:29.607 --> 00:02:32.277
were built as nation-building projects.

00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:34.821
These are cities that were experimental.

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:37.240
They were using a new type of aesthetic,

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:38.867
a new type of architecture

00:02:38.867 --> 00:02:40.451
to represent a country

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:42.912
that wanted to take a
totally new direction.

00:02:44.122 --> 00:02:47.458
And now, since the last 20, 25 years,

00:02:47.458 --> 00:02:50.170
new cities are seen as being

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.172
profit-making machines basically.

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:56.926
Some of these are even
private gated cities.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:59.721
It's almost like a gigantic mall,

00:02:59.721 --> 00:03:01.514
except it's the size of the city.

00:03:03.725 --> 00:03:05.560
There are other private cities

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.021
that are listed on stock exchanges

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:10.481
and they're answerable to shareholders.

00:03:10.481 --> 00:03:12.108
And so a tremendous amount

00:03:12.108 --> 00:03:14.152
is being invested in these projects

00:03:14.152 --> 00:03:17.155
in the hopes that they'll
attract foreign direct investment

00:03:17.155 --> 00:03:20.074
and sort of spark a new
type of digital economy.

00:03:23.328 --> 00:03:26.039
One of the most expensive
examples of this idea

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:28.666
is Songdo International Business District.

00:03:30.793 --> 00:03:33.755
The city is built on reclaimed
land in the Yellow Sea,

00:03:33.755 --> 00:03:37.091
just a two hour flight from
major destinations in Asia.

00:03:39.594 --> 00:03:42.764
The land reclamation
began in the mid-1990s

00:03:42.764 --> 00:03:45.558
and Songdo became an extension of Incheon,

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:47.435
a neighboring city whose government set up

00:03:47.435 --> 00:03:49.604
the Incheon Free Economic Zone

00:03:49.604 --> 00:03:52.148
to facilitate free trade
between South Korea

00:03:52.148 --> 00:03:53.650
and the rest of the world,

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:54.817
including China,

00:03:54.817 --> 00:03:56.402
which at that point wasn't a part

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:58.154
of the World Trade Organization.

00:04:05.536 --> 00:04:07.997
Songdo has a goal to become
an international city.

00:04:07.997 --> 00:04:10.124
So I think they might've purposely

00:04:10.124 --> 00:04:12.377
not to make it feel
like it's a Korean city.

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:17.215
The Incheon government contracted KPF,

00:04:17.215 --> 00:04:18.925
a New York-based architecture firm,

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:21.010
to design Songdo's master plan.

00:04:23.304 --> 00:04:26.683
Lead designer James von
Klemperer and over 60 architects

00:04:26.683 --> 00:04:29.477
spent eight years completing the project.

00:04:29.477 --> 00:04:32.355
There were hundreds of consultants,

00:04:32.355 --> 00:04:33.982
of engineers,

00:04:33.982 --> 00:04:34.983
designers,

00:04:34.983 --> 00:04:36.734
forecasters,

00:04:36.734 --> 00:04:38.194
material science people.

00:04:39.153 --> 00:04:42.240
So overall you probably had,

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.074
I would say,

00:04:43.074 --> 00:04:46.202
up to a thousand different
designers and consultants

00:04:46.202 --> 00:04:48.246
working on this city at one time.

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:55.295
We don't make a city without
any kind of templates

00:04:55.295 --> 00:04:59.299
and any kind of precedents that
inform us about urban life.

00:04:59.299 --> 00:05:00.133
As they say,

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:03.052
we stand on the shoulders of others.

00:05:03.052 --> 00:05:06.389
And rather than trying to
start with some new theory

00:05:06.389 --> 00:05:09.517
about human interaction
and building density

00:05:09.517 --> 00:05:11.352
and form and urban patterns,

00:05:11.352 --> 00:05:12.937
we borrowed, in a way,

00:05:14.022 --> 00:05:17.608
pieces of solutions from other cities.

00:05:17.608 --> 00:05:20.320
So we sort of pulled and pushed,

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.822
and like an artist making a collage

00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:25.241
with different preset elements,

00:05:25.241 --> 00:05:28.828
tried to use these pieces
of cities as inspirations

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:31.789
and borrow from some of
their dimensioning systems

00:05:31.789 --> 00:05:32.832
and strategies.

00:05:34.083 --> 00:05:37.545
These ideas were all woven
into Songdo's urban fabric,

00:05:37.545 --> 00:05:39.714
the physical structure of every city

00:05:39.714 --> 00:05:41.382
that defines its character.

00:05:41.382 --> 00:05:42.717
Things like building types,

00:05:42.717 --> 00:05:44.927
open spaces, roads, and lighting.

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:49.640
The team created neighborhoods
using a pattern of streets,

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:51.225
local stores,

00:05:51.225 --> 00:05:55.021
and a mix of low rise
buildings and towers,

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:57.190
all centered around a school and parks.

00:06:02.612 --> 00:06:04.947
When it comes to the
urban fabric of Songdo,

00:06:04.947 --> 00:06:07.533
we have a relatively large city block

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:11.204
and large avenues and a
lot of a really big scale

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:13.039
green areas and open spaces.

00:06:14.248 --> 00:06:17.377
Alberto Gonzalez is an
architect and urban planner

00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:20.088
and has been living in Songdo since 2017.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.801
One of the nice things
about Songdo urban design

00:06:24.801 --> 00:06:27.136
is that the blocks are very open.

00:06:27.136 --> 00:06:30.431
So you can actually walk
through all the blocks.

00:06:30.431 --> 00:06:32.558
Many of them have outdoor gyms,

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:34.394
others have playgrounds for kids.

00:06:34.394 --> 00:06:37.855
So it's literally open for
anyone who wants to go in.

00:06:38.815 --> 00:06:40.775
That's kind of a key distinction

00:06:40.775 --> 00:06:43.027
if you compare to other Asian cities

00:06:43.027 --> 00:06:45.613
in which you typically
have gated communities.

00:06:46.489 --> 00:06:49.951
So yeah, here is one of
the small pocket parks.

00:06:49.951 --> 00:06:52.995
And even though you are not too far

00:06:52.995 --> 00:06:54.414
from one of the main avenues,

00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:57.834
you feel like in a more protected site.

00:06:57.834 --> 00:07:00.962
I think it really makes a
city a bit more vibrant.

00:07:02.964 --> 00:07:06.926
One of the tenets that
we accepted or embraced

00:07:06.926 --> 00:07:11.514
in New Songdo City plan was
to make it a pedestrian city,

00:07:12.682 --> 00:07:14.809
a place where you'd be comfortable walking

00:07:14.809 --> 00:07:17.270
from district to district
or within districts.

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:20.148
But Korean planning and
Korean practice of building

00:07:20.148 --> 00:07:22.233
had embraced the automobile.

00:07:22.233 --> 00:07:23.860
Those who are working with us

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:26.320
from government ministry said,

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.281
"you must have an eight lane road

00:07:28.281 --> 00:07:32.076
at the edge of this part of town,

00:07:32.076 --> 00:07:34.078
or even going through the
middle of a neighborhood."

00:07:34.078 --> 00:07:38.666
We said, "well, that doesn't
sound very attractive."

00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:45.590
KPF couldn't stop the
highways being built.

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:48.134
So instead they focused
on how they were designed.

00:07:49.594 --> 00:07:51.179
Zoning restrictions in Korea

00:07:51.179 --> 00:07:53.598
mean that buildings
along these large avenues

00:07:53.598 --> 00:07:55.516
are usually set back from the street

00:07:55.516 --> 00:07:58.769
to protect residents
from noise and pollution.

00:07:58.769 --> 00:08:01.147
But the large sidewalks
created as a result

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:02.607
often lacked character,

00:08:02.607 --> 00:08:05.610
like textures on the
facades or shops and cafes

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.112
that make streets interesting
for people to use.

00:08:10.364 --> 00:08:12.533
And so we had to, in essence,

00:08:12.533 --> 00:08:16.037
break or change the Korean code

00:08:16.037 --> 00:08:19.165
to be able to have architectural textures

00:08:19.165 --> 00:08:21.083
and buildings right on the street.

00:08:22.001 --> 00:08:23.211
It would have been very difficult to do

00:08:23.211 --> 00:08:25.254
with a small one-off design,

00:08:25.254 --> 00:08:28.341
but the kind of scale of this project

00:08:28.341 --> 00:08:31.135
warranted that everybody
step back and think again

00:08:31.135 --> 00:08:34.764
about what made the
best kind of rules for building.

00:08:42.188 --> 00:08:43.940
Public transportation will get better,

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:46.734
but until then I'm going to use my car.

00:08:48.569 --> 00:08:50.530
Some elements of Songdo's design,

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:51.864
like reducing car use,

00:08:51.864 --> 00:08:53.991
may not be fully realized yet,

00:08:53.991 --> 00:08:56.786
but with 40% of the city left to be built

00:08:56.786 --> 00:08:58.287
there's a call-out for initiatives

00:08:58.287 --> 00:09:00.915
from the public on how to improve it.

00:09:00.915 --> 00:09:03.709
Alberto is consulting on
one of those projects.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:09.090
We are exploring ways in which we can

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:10.758
bring the space back to the people,

00:09:10.758 --> 00:09:12.760
reduce the usage of cars.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:16.764
So this is one of the spots
that people criticize a lot

00:09:16.764 --> 00:09:18.474
and, of course, with a lot of reasons.

00:09:18.474 --> 00:09:20.518
We have like a total of 10 lanes,

00:09:20.518 --> 00:09:23.604
five on each side, plus the buffer area.

00:09:23.604 --> 00:09:25.690
I mean, we literally have a huge avenue.

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:27.984
It's almost like a highway type of scale.

00:09:27.984 --> 00:09:30.653
So the idea is that in
this central section

00:09:30.653 --> 00:09:33.072
there is enough room to
put in a light rail here,

00:09:33.072 --> 00:09:34.031
like a tram,

00:09:34.031 --> 00:09:37.660
and maybe one or two
lanes at both sides

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:40.663
can get reduced and can be occupied

00:09:40.663 --> 00:09:43.791
with seating areas, with pop-up kiosks.

00:09:43.791 --> 00:09:45.459
So by getting rid of certain areas

00:09:45.459 --> 00:09:48.379
that are now purely used by cars,

00:09:48.379 --> 00:09:51.591
we can actually increase the chances

00:09:51.591 --> 00:09:54.468
for randomness to happen.

00:09:54.468 --> 00:09:56.679
So this type of urban randomness

00:09:56.679 --> 00:09:59.724
is actually what makes city
life much more interesting.

00:10:04.353 --> 00:10:06.272
Technology was another key element

00:10:06.272 --> 00:10:07.982
in Songdo's initial design

00:10:07.982 --> 00:10:10.401
and it became one of the
first smart city projects

00:10:10.401 --> 00:10:11.235
in the world.

00:10:12.486 --> 00:10:14.947
I see a lot of CCTV.

00:10:14.947 --> 00:10:16.741
If you go out on the street

00:10:16.741 --> 00:10:19.827
you don't see any power poles or wires,

00:10:19.827 --> 00:10:21.203
they're all underground.

00:10:26.667 --> 00:10:29.712
Cisco committed to spending $47 million

00:10:29.712 --> 00:10:32.089
to provide the city with
smart infrastructure.

00:10:34.008 --> 00:10:37.637
We designed systems that would ensure

00:10:37.637 --> 00:10:41.515
a highly efficient use of
energy and other resources,

00:10:41.515 --> 00:10:44.018
like water, even the use of waste.

00:10:44.018 --> 00:10:46.687
So this is the sort of thing you can do

00:10:46.687 --> 00:10:49.023
when you design a city from scratch

00:10:49.023 --> 00:10:52.360
because those infrastructural
systems are not there.

00:10:54.862 --> 00:10:57.073
This is Songdo's operation center,

00:10:57.073 --> 00:10:59.325
which manages all the infrastructure

00:10:59.325 --> 00:11:02.536
as well as monitors over
5,000 sensors and cameras

00:11:02.536 --> 00:11:03.663
spread around the city.

00:12:02.888 --> 00:12:04.432
A lot of these new city projects

00:12:04.432 --> 00:12:06.225
brand themselves as smart.

00:12:06.225 --> 00:12:10.062
They are trying to be the
Silicon Valley of Southeast Asia

00:12:10.062 --> 00:12:12.773
or the Silicon Valley of Latin America

00:12:12.773 --> 00:12:15.484
or the Silicon Valley
of Africa, et cetera.

00:12:15.484 --> 00:12:16.819
They seem to be inspired

00:12:16.819 --> 00:12:21.407
by this sort of entrepreneurial
stance of taking big risks,

00:12:21.407 --> 00:12:24.160
moving fast and breaking things.

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:26.203
But it's not just their
money that they're risking,

00:12:26.203 --> 00:12:27.413
they're risking land,

00:12:27.413 --> 00:12:29.665
they're risking the lives of residents

00:12:29.665 --> 00:12:31.751
who relocate to these places.

00:12:34.086 --> 00:12:35.796
The risk of failure is high

00:12:35.796 --> 00:12:39.091
with some new cities turning
into expensive ghost towns

00:12:39.091 --> 00:12:41.761
that aren't designed to
cater to the needs of locals.

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:46.766
Oftentimes locals are priced
out of these new cities

00:12:46.766 --> 00:12:48.726
because the new cities
are not built for them,

00:12:48.726 --> 00:12:49.977
they're built for investors.

00:12:49.977 --> 00:12:54.982
So we're seeing cities that
are only 10%, 20% occupied.

00:12:55.691 --> 00:12:56.984
The units are all sold,

00:12:56.984 --> 00:12:58.736
there's just nobody living in them.

00:12:59.945 --> 00:13:01.447
There are a lot of ghost cities in China

00:13:01.447 --> 00:13:03.157
that have been filled in.

00:13:03.157 --> 00:13:04.408
There are other ghost cities

00:13:04.408 --> 00:13:07.244
that are still very under-occupied.

00:13:07.244 --> 00:13:09.455
But whether these new
cities get built or not,

00:13:09.455 --> 00:13:10.664
whether they happen or not,

00:13:10.664 --> 00:13:11.916
whether they fill in or not,

00:13:11.916 --> 00:13:13.626
people are still gonna make money.

00:13:18.255 --> 00:13:21.425
Songdo seems to be defying
this ghost city trend.

00:13:21.425 --> 00:13:23.928
Its population is
tracking its development,

00:13:23.928 --> 00:13:28.265
now at about 60% of the
original target of 300,000,

00:13:28.265 --> 00:13:31.602
and there's an appetite
to replicate its success.

00:13:31.602 --> 00:13:33.062
The development has been described

00:13:33.062 --> 00:13:35.314
as an example of a "city in a box",

00:13:35.314 --> 00:13:37.316
with ready-made and tested solutions

00:13:37.316 --> 00:13:39.902
that can be quickly applied elsewhere.

00:13:39.902 --> 00:13:42.905
The same developer and architects
were contracted to design

00:13:42.905 --> 00:13:46.033
a new city in China called Meixi Lake.

00:13:46.033 --> 00:13:48.702
Its detailed master plan
took two and a half years

00:13:48.702 --> 00:13:50.204
while Songdo's took four.

00:13:51.914 --> 00:13:56.043
No city should ever be
a copy of another city

00:13:56.043 --> 00:14:01.048
because the particular
needs of place and time

00:14:01.048 --> 00:14:02.466
are so different.

00:14:02.466 --> 00:14:04.134
But,

00:14:04.134 --> 00:14:06.262
there are some techniques in city-building

00:14:06.262 --> 00:14:08.931
that have been taken
from one city to another

00:14:08.931 --> 00:14:10.724
quite successfully.

00:14:10.724 --> 00:14:13.644
And you could see if somebody
needs to build very quickly,

00:14:13.644 --> 00:14:16.856
they might want to
understand some templates

00:14:16.856 --> 00:14:18.941
of techniques that have been used before.

00:14:21.861 --> 00:14:24.488
I think Songdo is a successful story,

00:14:24.488 --> 00:14:25.573
it's definitely something

00:14:25.573 --> 00:14:28.617
that is gonna be a work in progress.

00:14:28.617 --> 00:14:32.872
And the typical question
is can we replicate this,

00:14:32.872 --> 00:14:36.041
can this "city in a box" type of environment

00:14:36.041 --> 00:14:38.752
be literally transferred to other areas?

00:14:40.254 --> 00:14:45.175
I think there's so many
ingredients that came into the mix

00:14:46.051 --> 00:14:48.971
for Songdo to happen
that I certainly think

00:14:48.971 --> 00:14:51.932
that it's not easy to replicate.

00:14:53.475 --> 00:14:56.854
This sort of notion that
we can build something

00:14:56.854 --> 00:14:58.939
that other people will want to emulate

00:14:58.939 --> 00:15:01.191
and we'll be the first,

00:15:01.191 --> 00:15:03.652
we'll be a model for other people to copy,

00:15:03.652 --> 00:15:07.031
that's a really appealing aspect

00:15:07.031 --> 00:15:08.365
of a lot of these new cities.

00:15:13.287 --> 00:15:16.832
Cities evolve over the
centuries and over the millennia

00:15:16.832 --> 00:15:20.502
into cities that we know today,

00:15:20.502 --> 00:15:25.049
Beijing or Delhi or other
major historical cities.

00:15:25.049 --> 00:15:26.425
And so, given time,

00:15:26.425 --> 00:15:30.262
maybe any new city can fill in and evolve

00:15:30.262 --> 00:15:33.766
and become something
lively and successful.

